Better Approach :-
1. Actually i approached this problem similar to insert function logic in array adt 
2. I created an vector and stored the values 
3. Shifted the values to their respective places and then filled the values stored in vector in their respective places 

Time-complexity:- O(N+k) = O(N)
Space-complexity:- O(N) in worst case

Optimised Approach:- ( Using the STL library Reverse function )
Optimised because of reduced space complexity
1. Reverse the whole array. 
2. Split the array into two parts at the given number(number of places to rotate)
3. Left side part of the splitted array only contains " k "(given number) elements
4. Reverse the left side array ,similarly reverse the right side array
5. Join the reversed arrays then the resultant array is the goal

Time-complexity:- O(2N) = O(N)
Space-complexity:- O(1)

My Code:- c++

class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        k = k % nums.size();
        vector<int> temp;
        for(int i = nums.size() - k; i < nums.size() ; i++){
             temp.emplace_back(nums[i]);
        }
        for(int i = nums.size()-k-1; i >= 0 ; i--){
            nums[i+k] = nums[i];
        }
        for(int i = 0 ; i<k ; i++){
            nums[i] = temp[i];
        }
    }
};
